# PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS

## Contents of this Part

## SECTION I Interim Remedies in General

## 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (a)an interim injunction;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (b)an interim declaration;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (c)an order for—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (i)the detention, custody or preservation of relevant property;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (ii)the inspection of relevant property;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (iii)the taking of a sample of relevant property;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (iv)the carrying out of an experiment on or with relevant property;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (v)the sale of relevant property of a perishable nature, or where sale is urgent for any other reason; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (vi)the payment of income from relevant property until a claim is decided;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (d)an order authorising a person to enter any land or building in the possession of a party to the proceedings for the purposes of carrying out an order under sub-paragraph (c);

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (e)an order under section 4 of the Torts (Interference with Goods) Act 1977( 1 ) to deliver up goods;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (f)an order (‘freezing injunction’)—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (i)restraining a party from removing assets from the jurisdiction; or

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (ii)restraining a party from dealing with any assets whether located within the jurisdiction or not;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (g)an order directing a party to provide information about relevant property or assets, including their location, which are or may be the subject of an application for a freezing injunction;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (h)an order (‘search order’) under section 7 of the Civil Procedure Act 1997 (order requiring a party to admit another party to premises for the purpose of preserving evidence etc.);

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (i)an order (‘imaging order’) under section IV of this Part for an independent IT expert to be given access to any electronic data storage devices or online accounts for the purpose of copying the contents;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (j)an order under section 33 of the Senior Courts Act 1981( 2 ) or section 52 of the County Courts Act 1984( 3 ) (order for disclosure of documents or inspection of property before a claim has been made);

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (k)an order under section 34 of the Senior Courts Act 1981 or section 53 of the County Courts Act 1984 (order in certain proceedings for disclosure of documents or inspection of property against a non-party);

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (l)an order (‘order for interim payment’) under section V of this Part for payment by a defendant on account of any sum (except costs) which the court may hold the defendant liable to pay;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (m)an order for a specified fund to be paid into court or otherwise secured, where there is a dispute over a party’s right to the fund;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (n)an order permitting a party seeking to recover personal property to pay money into court pending the outcome of the proceedings and directing that, if they do so, the property shall be given up to them;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (o)an order directing a party to prepare and file accounts relating to the dispute;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (p)an order directing any account to be taken or inquiry to be made by the court; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (q)an order for security for costs. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (2) In paragraph (1)(c) and (g), ‘relevant property’ means property (including land) which is the subject of a claim or as to which any question may arise on a claim. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (3) The fact that an interim remedy is not listed in paragraph (1) does not affect any power the court may have to grant that remedy. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (4) The court may grant an interim remedy whether or not there has been a claim for a final remedy of that kind. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (5) Section II of this Part contains additional provisions relating to interim injunctions. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (6) Sections III to VI of this Part contain additional provisions relating to specific types of interim remedy. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] 25.2. —(1) An order for an interim remedy may be made at any time, including before proceedings are started or after judgment has been given, subject to any rule, practice direction or enactment which provides otherwise. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (2) The court may grant an interim remedy before a claim has been started only if the matter is urgent, or it is otherwise desirable to do so in the interests of justice. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (3) A defendant may not apply for an interim remedy before filing either an acknowledgment of service or a defence, unless the court directs otherwise. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (4) Where the court grants an interim remedy before a claim has been started, it must give directions requiring a claim to be commenced, unless—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (a)the application is made under section 33 of the Senior Courts Act 1981 or section 52 of the County Courts Act 1984; or

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (b)the court considers it is inappropriate. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] 25.3. —(1) An application for an interim remedy must be supported by evidence, unless the court directs otherwise. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (2) The court may grant an interim remedy on an application made without notice if it appears to the court that there are good reasons for not giving notice. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (3) Evidence in support of an application made without notice must state the reasons why notice has not been given. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.1. —(1) Interim remedies include—] (Part 23 contains general rules about making an application.)

## 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (a)in relation to actual or intended proceedings outside the jurisdiction; or

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (b)under section 33 of the Senior Courts Act 1981 or section 52 of the County Courts Act 1984 (order for disclosure, inspection etc. before commencement) before a claim has been commenced,

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] the application must be made in accordance with the general rules about applications contained in Part 23. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (2) Paragraphs (3) and (4) apply where a person makes an application under—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (a)section 33(1) of the Senior Courts Act 1981 or section 52(1) of the County Courts Act 1984 (inspection etc. of property before commencement); or

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (b)section 34(3) of the Senior Courts Act 1981 or section 53(3) of the County Courts Act 1984 (inspection etc. of property against a non-party). [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (3) The evidence supporting such an application must show that the property—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (a)is or may become the subject matter of such actual or anticipated proceedings; or

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (b)is relevant to the issues that will arise in such proceedings. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.4. —(1) Where a person wishes to apply for an interim remedy—] (4) A copy of the application notice and of the supporting evidence must be served on the person against whom the order is sought, and in relation to an application under section 34(3) of the Senior Courts Act 1981 or section 53(3) of the County Courts Act 1984, on every party to the proceedings other than the applicant. ## SECTION II Interim Injunctions

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION II Interim Injunctions] 25.5. —(1) In the High Court, Masters and District Judges may grant interim injunctions by consent, or in connection with charging orders and appointments of receivers, or in support of execution of judgments. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION II Interim Injunctions] (2) In any other case, any judge who has jurisdiction to conduct the trial of the action may grant an interim injunction in that action. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION II Interim Injunctions] (3) Masters or District Judges have the power to vary or discharge an interim injunction granted by any judge, if all the parties consent. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION II Interim Injunctions] (4) High Court Judges or any other judges duly authorised may grant search orders and freezing injunctions. ## 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (2) Save where the applicant reasonably believes that there is good reason for not giving notice, the applicant must serve the application notice, evidence in support, draft order and notice of the date, time and place of the hearing as soon as possible after filing and in any event not less than 3 days before the hearing date for the application. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (3) Where an application is made on paper, sufficient copies of the application notice and evidence in support for the court and for each respondent must be filed for issue and service. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (4) Whenever possible, the applicant must—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (a)file a draft of the order sought with the application notice; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (b)provide an electronic version of the draft order to the court, in a format compatible with enabling the draft to be amended by the court. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] 25.7. —(1) Applications must be supported by evidence in the following form unless the court, a rule, a practice direction or an enactment requires an affidavit or affirmation—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (a)a witness statement;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (b)a statement of case; or

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (c)the application. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (2) The evidence must set out all relevant facts. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] 25.8. —(1) Where the applicant reasonably believes that there is good reason for not giving the required notice, and where the application is to be dealt with at a court hearing—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (a)the application notice, supporting evidence and a draft order must be filed with the court at least two hours before the hearing wherever possible;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (b)if an application is made before the application notice has been issued, a draft order must be provided at the hearing, and the application notice and evidence in support must be filed with the court on the same or next working day or as ordered by the court;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (c)except in cases where the applicant reasonably believes that secrecy is essential, the applicant must take steps to notify the respondent of the application; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (d)the applicant must take or arrange to be taken a note of the hearing and serve this on the respondent. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (2) Where the application is to be dealt with at a court hearing before the issue of a claim form, in addition to the requirements of paragraph (1)—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (a)unless the court orders otherwise, either the applicant must undertake to the court to issue a claim form immediately or the court must give directions for the commencement of the claim;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (b)where possible the claim form must be served with the order for the injunction; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (c)the order must state in the title after the names of the applicant and respondent ‘the Claimant and Defendant in an Intended Action’. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (3) Where the application is to be dealt with remotely—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (a)the applicant must be prepared to send the judge a draft order in electronic format; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (b)the application notice and evidence in support must be filed with the court on the day of the hearing or next working day or as ordered, together with two copies of the order for sealing where the application is made on paper. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (Further information about the procedure for urgent applications, including urgent out of hours applications, may be found in the relevant court guide, on the HMCTS website, or by contacting the court directly.)

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] 25.9. —(1) An order for an interim injunction must set out clearly what the respondent must do or not do. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (2) An order for an interim injunction made in the presence of all parties to be bound by it or made at a hearing of which they have had notice, may state that it is effective until trial or further order. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (3) Unless the court orders otherwise, an order for an interim injunction must contain—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (a)subject to paragraph (6), an undertaking by the applicant to the court to pay any damages which the respondent sustains and which the court considers the applicant should pay;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (b)if made without notice to any other party, an undertaking by the applicant to the court to serve on the respondent the application notice, evidence in support, note of the hearing and any order made as soon as practicable;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (c)if made without notice to any other party, a return date for a further hearing at which the other party can be present;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (d)if made before filing the application notice, an undertaking to file and pay the appropriate fee on the same or next working day; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (e)if made before issue of a claim form, an undertaking to issue and pay the appropriate fee on the same or next working day, or directions for the commencement of the claim. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (4) When the court makes an order for delivery up or preservation of evidence or property which is likely to be executed at the premises of the respondent or a third party, it must consider whether to include provisions for the benefit or protection of those parties. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (5) Subject to paragraph (6), when the court makes an order for an interim injunction, it must consider whether to require an undertaking by the applicant to pay any damages sustained by a person other than the respondent, including another party to the proceedings or any other person who may suffer loss as a consequence of the order. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (6) In an Aarhus Convention claim to which rules 46.24 to 46.28 apply, if the court is satisfied that an interim injunction is necessary to prevent significant environmental damage and to preserve the factual basis of the proceedings, the court must, in considering whether to require an undertaking by the applicant, and the terms of any such undertaking—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (a)have regard to the need for the terms of the order not to make continuing with the claim prohibitively expensive for the applicant; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (b)give such directions as are necessary to ensure that the case is heard promptly. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (7) In this Section ‘Aarhus Convention claim’ has the same meaning as in rule 46.24(2). [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (8) Proceedings are ‘prohibitively expensive’ if their likely costs, including any court fees payable by the applicant and the amount of any cross-undertaking in damages, and having regard to any limit under Part 46 on a party’s maximum costs liability, either exceed the financial resources of the applicant, or are objectively unreasonable having regard to the factors set out in rule 46.27(3)(b). [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (9) When a court considers the financial resources of the applicant, it must have regard to any financial support which any person has provided or is likely to provide to the applicant. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] 25.10. —(1) If a claim is stayed other than by agreement between the parties, any interim injunction (other than a freezing injunction) granted in or in anticipation of the claim shall cease to have effect, unless the court orders otherwise. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (2) If a claim is automatically struck out, any interim injunction granted to the claimant in or in anticipation of the claim shall cease to have effect 14 days after the claim is struck out, unless the court orders otherwise or paragraph (3) applies. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (3) If a claimant applies within 14 days to reinstate a claim that has been automatically struck out, any interim injunction granted to them in or in anticipation of the claim shall continue until the hearing of the application, unless the court orders otherwise. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (Rules 20.2 and 20.3 provide that counterclaims and other additional claims are treated as claims and that references to a claimant include a party bringing an additional claim.)

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] 25.11. —(1) The following provisions apply to orders which will affect a person, other than the applicant or respondent, who is served with the order but did not attend the hearing at which the order was made. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (2) Where such a person requests—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (a)a copy of any material provided to the judge, including material prepared after the hearing at the direction of the judge or in compliance with the order; or

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] (b)a note of the hearing,

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.6. —(1) The application notice must state the order sought.] the applicant or their legal representative must comply with the request forthwith, unless the court orders otherwise. ## SECTION III Freezing Injunctions

## 25.12. This Section applies to applications for freezing injunctions. ## 25.13. An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.13. An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] 25.14. —(1) The applicant must use the wording of the model order in the form approved in accordance with rule 4(1), modified as appropriate. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.13. An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] (2) Any modifications to the model order must be drawn to the judge’s attention at the application hearing. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.13. An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] (3) The court may, if it considers it appropriate, require the applicant’s solicitors, as well as the applicant, to give undertakings. ## SECTION IV Search and Imaging Orders

## 25.15. This Section applies to applications for search orders, imaging orders, or both. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.15. This Section applies to applications for search orders, imaging orders, or both.] 25.16. In this Section, ‘Supervising Solicitor’ means a solicitor experienced in the operation of search orders, who is not an employee or member of the applicant’s firm of solicitors. ## 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] (2) The affidavit or affirmation must—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] (a)state the address of the premises and whether it is a private or business address;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] (b)state the name, firm and its address, and experience of the Supervising Solicitor and, where an imaging order is sought, the independent IT expert; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] (c)fully disclose the reason the order is sought, including the probability that relevant material would disappear if the order were not made. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] 25.18. —(1) The applicant must use the wording of the model search and imaging order in the form approved in accordance with rule 4(1), modified as appropriate. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] (2) Any modifications to the model order must be drawn to the judge’s attention at the application hearing. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] (3) If the court orders that service by the Supervising Solicitor is not required, the reasons must be set out in the order. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.17. —(1) An application must be supported by evidence on affidavit or affirmation.] 25.19. Unless the court otherwise orders, the order must be served personally by a Supervising Solicitor. ## SECTION V Interim Payment Orders

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] 25.20. —(1) The court must not order an interim payment of more than a reasonable proportion of the likely amount of the final judgment. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (2) The court must take into account contributory negligence and any relevant set-off or counterclaim. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (3) The court may order an interim payment by instalments. If so, the order must set out—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (a)the total amount of the payment;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (b)the amount of each instalment;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (c)the number of instalments and the date on which each is to be paid; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (d)to whom payment should be made. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (4) Where a party seeks an interim payment and the court has ordered an account to be taken, if on the evidence the account is bound to result in a payment to the applicant, the court must, before making an order for interim payment, order that the liable party pay to the applicant the amount shown by the account to be due.
